msgid "Rebuild the start-up files"
msgstr "Rebuild the start-up files"

msgid "Copies fresh start-up files from this Windows to the start-up partition, writes a new start-up entry for it and puts it first."
msgstr "Copies fresh start-up files from this Windows to the start-up partition, writes a new start-up entry for it and puts it first."

msgid "About a minute. Personal files are not touched. The old start-up files are backed up first."
msgstr "About a minute. Personal files are not touched. The old start-up files are backed up first."

msgid "Adds this Windows to the menu shown when the PC starts, after the systems that are already there."
msgstr "Adds this Windows to the menu shown when the PC starts, after the systems that are already there."

msgid "About a minute. The other systems keep starting as before."
msgstr "About a minute. The other systems keep starting as before."

msgid "Show it in the start-up menu"
msgstr "Show it in the start-up menu"

msgid "This Windows has a start-up entry, but the entry is hidden from the menu. This puts it back in the menu."
msgstr "This Windows has a start-up entry, but the entry is hidden from the menu. This puts it back in the menu."

msgid "A few seconds."
msgstr "A few seconds."

msgid "Makes this Windows the one that starts when nobody chooses in the start-up menu."
msgstr "Makes this Windows the one that starts when nobody chooses in the start-up menu."

msgid "Mark the start-up partition as active"
msgstr "Mark the start-up partition as active"

msgid "Older PCs (legacy BIOS) only start from the partition marked \"active\". This sets that mark on the right partition."
msgstr "Older PCs (legacy BIOS) only start from the partition marked \"active\". This sets that mark on the right partition."

msgid "A few seconds. The partition table is backed up first."
msgstr "A few seconds. The partition table is backed up first."

msgid "Write new start-up code to the disk"
msgstr "Write new start-up code to the disk"

msgid "Writes fresh start-up code to the first sector of the disk and of the start-up partition, so an older PC (legacy BIOS) can start from it."
msgstr "Writes fresh start-up code to the first sector of the disk and of the start-up partition, so an older PC (legacy BIOS) can start from it."

msgid "A few seconds. The old first sectors are backed up first. Other systems' start-up menus (for example Linux GRUB) on this disk are replaced."
msgstr "A few seconds. The old first sectors are backed up first. Other systems' start-up menus (for example Linux GRUB) on this disk are replaced."

msgid "Create a new start-up partition"
msgstr "Create a new start-up partition"

msgid "Makes a small start-up partition (300 MB) in free space on the disk, taking the room from the end of the Windows partition if needed, and puts fresh start-up files on it."
msgstr "Makes a small start-up partition (300 MB) in free space on the disk, taking the room from the end of the Windows partition if needed, and puts fresh start-up files on it."

msgid "A few minutes. The Windows partition gets 300 MB smaller. Copy important files somewhere safe first if you can."
msgstr "A few minutes. The Windows partition gets 300 MB smaller. Copy important files somewhere safe first if you can."

msgid "Repair the partition table"
msgstr "Repair the partition table"

msgid "Writes both copies of the partition table again from the copy that Windows can still read. The partitions themselves are not moved."
msgstr "Writes both copies of the partition table again from the copy that Windows can still read. The partitions themselves are not moved."

msgid "A few seconds. The old table is backed up first."
msgstr "A few seconds. The old table is backed up first."

msgid "Check and repair the drive"
msgstr "Check and repair the drive"

msgid "Runs the Windows disk check, which repairs damaged file-system records. Files in damaged places can be lost, as with any disk check."
msgstr "Runs the Windows disk check, which repairs damaged file-system records. Files in damaged places can be lost, as with any disk check."

msgid "From a few minutes to an hour on a large drive. This cannot be undone."
msgstr "From a few minutes to an hour on a large drive. This cannot be undone."

msgid "Unlock with the BitLocker recovery key"
msgstr "Unlock with the BitLocker recovery key"

msgid "This drive is encrypted. Type its 48-digit recovery key to unlock it, so it can be checked and repaired. You find the key in your Microsoft account (aka.ms/myrecoverykey), on a printout, or with your IT department."
msgstr "This drive is encrypted. Type its 48-digit recovery key to unlock it, so it can be checked and repaired. You find the key in your Microsoft account (aka.ms/myrecoverykey), on a printout, or with your IT department."

msgid "A few seconds. Nothing is changed on the drive."
msgstr "A few seconds. Nothing is changed on the drive."

msgid "Put back the registry's backup copy"
msgstr "Put back the registry's backup copy"

msgid "Windows' settings file (registry) is damaged. Windows keeps an automatic backup copy of it, and this puts that copy back. Settings changed since the backup was made are lost."
msgstr "Windows' settings file (registry) is damaged. Windows keeps an automatic backup copy of it, and this puts that copy back. Settings changed since the backup was made are lost."

msgid "A few seconds. The damaged file is kept in the backup, so it can be put back too."
msgstr "A few seconds. The damaged file is kept in the backup, so it can be put back too."

msgid "Repair Windows system files"
msgstr "Repair Windows system files"

msgid "Runs the Windows file checker, which puts back damaged or missing Windows files from Windows' own store of originals."
msgstr "Runs the Windows file checker, which puts back damaged or missing Windows files from Windows' own store of originals."

msgid "10 to 30 minutes. Personal files and programs are not touched. This cannot be undone, but it only puts back Windows' own files."
msgstr "10 to 30 minutes. Personal files and programs are not touched. This cannot be undone, but it only puts back Windows' own files."

msgid "Turn the disk drivers back on"
msgstr "Turn the disk drivers back on"

msgid "Windows needs a few drivers to read its own disk while it starts. Some of them are turned off, which causes the stop error INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE. This turns them back on."
msgstr "Windows needs a few drivers to read its own disk while it starts. Some of them are turned off, which causes the stop error INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE. This turns them back on."

msgid "A few seconds. The settings file is backed up first."
msgstr "A few seconds. The settings file is backed up first."

msgid "Clear the unfinished set-up mark"
msgstr "Clear the unfinished set-up mark"

msgid "Windows is marked as still being set up, so it keeps restarting into set-up (\"The computer restarted unexpectedly\"). This clears the mark. Only do this on a Windows that was already working before."
msgstr "Windows is marked as still being set up, so it keeps restarting into set-up (\"The computer restarted unexpectedly\"). This clears the mark. Only do this on a Windows that was already working before."

msgid "Windows updates that were half installed can make Windows restart over and over. This undoes the updates that are still waiting to finish."
msgstr "Windows updates that were half installed can make Windows restart over and over. This undoes the updates that are still waiting to finish."

msgid "5 to 20 minutes. Those updates can be installed again later from Windows Update."
msgstr "5 to 20 minutes. Those updates can be installed again later from Windows Update."

msgid "Show the real error instead of Automatic Repair"
msgstr "Show the real error instead of Automatic Repair"

msgid "Turns off Automatic Repair for this Windows, so the PC stops going round in circles and shows the actual error message on screen. You can turn it back on with Undo."
msgstr "Turns off Automatic Repair for this Windows, so the PC stops going round in circles and shows the actual error message on screen. You can turn it back on with Undo."

msgid "Move Windows back to the top of the drive"
msgstr "Move Windows back to the top of the drive"

msgid "This Windows sits inside a folder, where it cannot start. This moves its folders (Windows, Program Files, Users and the rest) back to the top of the drive, where they belong."
msgstr "This Windows sits inside a folder, where it cannot start. This moves its folders (Windows, Program Files, Users and the rest) back to the top of the drive, where they belong."

msgid "A few seconds: the files are only renamed, not copied. Undo moves them back."
msgstr "A few seconds: the files are only renamed, not copied. Undo moves them back."

msgid "Remove this entry"
msgstr "Remove this entry"

msgid "Deletes this line from the start-up settings, so it no longer appears in the start-up menu. No Windows, files or partitions are touched."
msgstr "Deletes this line from the start-up settings, so it no longer appears in the start-up menu. No Windows, files or partitions are touched."

msgid "A few seconds. The start-up settings are backed up first, so Undo brings the entry back."
msgstr "A few seconds. The start-up settings are backed up first, so Undo brings the entry back."

msgid "Only hide it from the menu"
msgstr "Only hide it from the menu"

msgid "Keeps the entry in the start-up settings but takes it off the start-up menu. Choose this if you think the system may come back (for example a disk you will connect again)."
msgstr "Keeps the entry in the start-up settings but takes it off the start-up menu. Choose this if you think the system may come back (for example a disk you will connect again)."

msgid "A few seconds. Undo puts it back in the menu."
msgstr "A few seconds. Undo puts it back in the menu."

msgid "Restart into the PC's firmware settings"
msgstr "Restart into the PC's firmware settings"

msgid "Restarts the PC straight into its firmware settings screen (where Secure Boot, the start-up order and CSM are switched). Close your programs first."
msgstr "Restarts the PC straight into its firmware settings screen (where Secure Boot, the start-up order and CSM are switched). Close your programs first."

msgid "The PC restarts at once. Nothing on the disks changes."
msgstr "The PC restarts at once. Nothing on the disks changes."

msgid "Add Linux to the PC's start-up list"
msgstr "Add Linux to the PC's start-up list"

msgid "Writes a new entry into the start-up list kept inside the PC, pointing to this Linux's boot loader. Nothing on any disk changes."
msgstr "Writes a new entry into the start-up list kept inside the PC, pointing to this Linux's boot loader. Nothing on any disk changes."

msgid "A few seconds. Undo removes the entry again."
msgstr "A few seconds. Undo removes the entry again."

msgid "Make Linux start first"
msgstr "Make Linux start first"

msgid "Moves this Linux to the top of the PC's start-up list. Its boot loader (GRUB) usually shows a menu where Windows can still be chosen."
msgstr "Moves this Linux to the top of the PC's start-up list. Its boot loader (GRUB) usually shows a menu where Windows can still be chosen."

msgid "A few seconds. Undo puts the old order back."
msgstr "A few seconds. Undo puts the old order back."

msgid "Remove the leftover boot loader"
msgstr "Remove the leftover boot loader"

msgid "Deletes the boot loader folder of a Linux that is gone from the start-up partition, and its line in the PC's start-up list."
msgstr "Deletes the boot loader folder of a Linux that is gone from the start-up partition, and its line in the PC's start-up list."

msgid "A few seconds. The folder and the entry are backed up first, so Undo brings them back."
msgstr "A few seconds. The folder and the entry are backed up first, so Undo brings them back."

msgid "Repair Linux from a Linux USB stick"
msgstr "Repair Linux from a Linux USB stick"

msgid "This needs to be done from Linux itself:"
msgstr "This needs to be done from Linux itself:"

msgid "About 15 minutes with a Linux USB stick and an internet connection."
msgstr "About 15 minutes with a Linux USB stick and an internet connection."

msgid "Switch the troubleshoot options back on"
msgstr "Switch the troubleshoot options back on"

msgid "Windows' repair tools (the blue \"Choose an option\" screen with Troubleshoot, Startup Repair and System Restore) are on this PC but switched off. This switches them on again with Windows' own tool (reagentc)."
msgstr "Windows' repair tools (the blue \"Choose an option\" screen with Troubleshoot, Startup Repair and System Restore) are on this PC but switched off. This switches them on again with Windows' own tool (reagentc)."

msgid "A few seconds. No partition changes."
msgstr "A few seconds. No partition changes."

msgid "Recreate the recovery partition"
msgstr "Recreate the recovery partition"

msgid "Makes a new hidden recovery partition (about 1 GB) and puts Windows' repair tools on it, so the blue \"Choose an option\" screen with Troubleshoot, Startup Repair and System Restore works again. Uses unused space or an empty recovery partition if there is one; otherwise it takes 1 GB from the end of the Windows partition."
msgstr "Makes a new hidden recovery partition (about 1 GB) and puts Windows' repair tools on it, so the blue \"Choose an option\" screen with Troubleshoot, Startup Repair and System Restore works again. Uses unused space or an empty recovery partition if there is one; otherwise it takes 1 GB from the end of the Windows partition."

msgid "A few minutes. Making the Windows partition smaller is safe in almost all cases, but a power cut during it can damage the drive: back up important files first, and keep a laptop plugged in. This cannot be undone here."
msgstr "A few minutes. Making the Windows partition smaller is safe in almost all cases, but a power cut during it can damage the drive: back up important files first, and keep a laptop plugged in. This cannot be undone here."

msgid "Free up space"
msgstr "Free up space"

msgid "Removes files Windows does not need: temporary files, downloaded updates, crash reports and, if you choose, browser caches. You see how much each frees before anything is removed."
msgstr "Removes files Windows does not need: temporary files, downloaded updates, crash reports and, if you choose, browser caches. You see how much each frees before anything is removed."

msgid "A minute or two. Personal files, programs and settings are not touched. Removed files cannot be put back (none of them are needed)."
msgstr "A minute or two. Personal files, programs and settings are not touched. Removed files cannot be put back (none of them are needed)."

msgid "Make the Windows partition bigger"
msgstr "Make the Windows partition bigger"

msgid "Adds unused space right after the Windows partition to it, or, if you choose, deletes the partition right after it and adds that space. Space before it cannot be used. You see a preview of the disk before anything changes."
msgstr "Adds unused space right after the Windows partition to it, or, if you choose, deletes the partition right after it and adds that space. Space before it cannot be used. You see a preview of the disk before anything changes."

msgid "A minute. Changing partitions always carries a small risk: back up important files first. Deleting the partition after it erases everything on that partition."
msgstr "A minute. Changing partitions always carries a small risk: back up important files first. Deleting the partition after it erases everything on that partition."

msgid "Give Windows' folders their names back"
msgstr "Give Windows' folders their names back"

msgid "Windows only starts from folders with their own names (Windows, System32, config). This renames the folders found back to those names. Nothing is copied or deleted."
msgstr "Windows only starts from folders with their own names (Windows, System32, config). This renames the folders found back to those names. Nothing is copied or deleted."

msgid "A few seconds. Undo renames them back."
msgstr "A few seconds. Undo renames them back."

msgid "Create the reserved partition (MSR)"
msgstr "Create the reserved partition (MSR)"

msgid "Makes the small 16 MB reserved partition Windows expects on a GPT disk, right after the start-up partition when there is room there, otherwise at the end of the Windows partition."
msgstr "Makes the small 16 MB reserved partition Windows expects on a GPT disk, right after the start-up partition when there is room there, otherwise at the end of the Windows partition."

msgid "A few seconds. If there is no unused space, the Windows partition gives up 16 MB. The partition table is backed up first."
msgstr "A few seconds. If there is no unused space, the Windows partition gives up 16 MB. The partition table is backed up first."

msgid "Put the partitions in Windows' own order…"
msgstr "Put the partitions in Windows' own order…"

msgid "Opens a plan that puts this disk's partitions in the order Windows Setup makes them (start-up, reserved, Windows, recovery) by moving and resizing them, step by step, each one shown and confirmed first. Only from the rescue USB stick or another Windows, never on the disk in use."
msgstr "Opens a plan that puts this disk's partitions in the order Windows Setup makes them (start-up, reserved, Windows, recovery) by moving and resizing them, step by step, each one shown and confirmed first. Only from the rescue USB stick or another Windows, never on the disk in use."

msgid "From minutes to hours, depending on how much must be moved. Back up important files first."
msgstr "From minutes to hours, depending on how much must be moved. Back up important files first."

msgid "Repair the Windows image (DISM)"
msgstr "Repair the Windows image (DISM)"

msgid "Checks Windows' store of its own components and puts back damaged ones. The running Windows takes good copies from Windows Update; a Windows that does not start takes them from Windows install media of the same version (install.wim or install.esd). Run \"Repair Windows system files\" afterwards."
msgstr "Checks Windows' store of its own components and puts back damaged ones. The running Windows takes good copies from Windows Update; a Windows that does not start takes them from Windows install media of the same version (install.wim or install.esd). Run \"Repair Windows system files\" afterwards."

msgid "15 to 60 minutes. Personal files and programs are not touched. This cannot be undone, but it only puts back Windows' own parts."
msgstr "15 to 60 minutes. Personal files and programs are not touched. This cannot be undone, but it only puts back Windows' own parts."

msgid "Remove a recent update…"
msgstr "Remove a recent update…"

msgid "Shows the updates installed last and removes the one you choose. If Windows started failing right after an update, removing that update often makes it start again."
msgstr "Shows the updates installed last and removes the one you choose. If Windows started failing right after an update, removing that update often makes it start again."

msgid "A few minutes. Windows Update may offer the update again later; pause updates for a while if it caused trouble."
msgstr "A few minutes. Windows Update may offer the update again later; pause updates for a while if it caused trouble."

msgid "Start in Safe Mode next time"
msgstr "Start in Safe Mode next time"

msgid "Makes this Windows start in Safe Mode (only the most basic drivers) until it is switched back, so a faulty driver or program can be removed. Switch it back with \"Normal start again\"."
msgstr "Makes this Windows start in Safe Mode (only the most basic drivers) until it is switched back, so a faulty driver or program can be removed. Switch it back with \"Normal start again\"."

msgid "A few seconds. Undo switches it back."
msgstr "A few seconds. Undo switches it back."

msgid "Switch this driver off"
msgstr "Switch this driver off"

msgid "Stops Windows from loading this driver when it starts (its Start value is set to 4, in every control set). The driver's files stay. Use it for a driver of another maker that makes Windows crash while starting; never for Microsoft's own disk and file-system drivers."
msgstr "Stops Windows from loading this driver when it starts (its Start value is set to 4, in every control set). The driver's files stay. Use it for a driver of another maker that makes Windows crash while starting; never for Microsoft's own disk and file-system drivers."

msgid "A few seconds. The settings file is backed up first, so Undo switches it on again."
msgstr "A few seconds. The settings file is backed up first, so Undo switches it on again."

msgid "Add a driver to this Windows"
msgstr "Add a driver to this Windows"

msgid "Puts a driver (its .inf, .sys and .cat files) into this Windows' own driver store, so Windows loads it the next time it starts. This is what a Windows needs when it stops with INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE because it has no driver for its disk controller (Intel VMD / RST, RAID)."
msgstr "Puts a driver (its .inf, .sys and .cat files) into this Windows' own driver store, so Windows loads it the next time it starts. This is what a Windows needs when it stops with INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE because it has no driver for its disk controller (Intel VMD / RST, RAID)."

msgid "About a minute. The driver must be 64-bit and made for this Windows version. This cannot be undone here (the driver can be removed in Windows' Device Manager)."
msgstr "About a minute. The driver must be 64-bit and made for this Windows version. This cannot be undone here (the driver can be removed in Windows' Device Manager)."

msgid "Change a start-up option"
msgstr "Change a start-up option"

msgid "Changes how this Windows starts the next time."
msgstr "Changes how this Windows starts the next time."

msgid "A few seconds. Undo puts the old setting back."
msgstr "A few seconds. Undo puts the old setting back."

msgid "Save the BitLocker recovery keys now…"
msgstr "Save the BitLocker recovery keys now…"

msgid "Reads the 48-digit recovery key of every unlocked BitLocker drive while Windows is running (Windows can show it now; once Windows does not start, it cannot), and saves them next to this program, locked with your password."
msgstr "Reads the 48-digit recovery key of every unlocked BitLocker drive while Windows is running (Windows can show it now; once Windows does not start, it cannot), and saves them next to this program, locked with your password."

msgid "A few seconds. Nothing on the drives changes."
msgstr "A few seconds. Nothing on the drives changes."

msgid "Rename it in the start-up menu"
msgstr "Rename it in the start-up menu"

msgid "Changes the name shown for this Windows in the start-up menu, so several Windows can be told apart."
msgstr "Changes the name shown for this Windows in the start-up menu, so several Windows can be told apart."

msgid "A few seconds. Undo puts the old name back."
msgstr "A few seconds. Undo puts the old name back."

msgid "Set how long the start-up menu waits"
msgstr "Set how long the start-up menu waits"

msgid "Sets how many seconds the start-up menu is shown before the first Windows starts by itself."
msgstr "Sets how many seconds the start-up menu is shown before the first Windows starts by itself."

msgid "A few seconds. Undo puts the old time back."
msgstr "A few seconds. Undo puts the old time back."

msgid "Bring back the Windows in Windows.old"
msgstr "Bring back the Windows in Windows.old"

msgid "Puts the earlier Windows kept in Windows.old back in place: the current Windows, Program Files, ProgramData and Users folders are moved into a new folder (Windows.replaced), the ones in Windows.old are moved to the top of the drive, and fresh start-up files are written for it."
msgstr "Puts the earlier Windows kept in Windows.old back in place: the current Windows, Program Files, ProgramData and Users folders are moved into a new folder (Windows.replaced), the ones in Windows.old are moved to the top of the drive, and fresh start-up files are written for it."

msgid "A minute: folders are only moved, not copied. Programs installed and settings changed since the update are lost (they stay in Windows.replaced). Undo moves everything back."
msgstr "A minute: folders are only moved, not copied. Programs installed and settings changed since the update are lost (they stay in Windows.replaced). Undo moves everything back."

msgid "Remove it from the PC's start-up list"
msgstr "Remove it from the PC's start-up list"

msgid "Deletes this line from the list of start-up choices kept inside the PC itself (not on any disk). Nothing on any disk changes."
msgstr "Deletes this line from the list of start-up choices kept inside the PC itself (not on any disk). Nothing on any disk changes."

msgid "A few seconds. The entry is backed up first, so Undo brings it back."
msgstr "A few seconds. The entry is backed up first, so Undo brings it back."

msgid "Starting a PC is like a relay race: each runner hands a baton to the next. First the PC's built-in program (firmware) wakes up and looks at the disk. On the disk it finds a small start-up partition with Windows Boot Manager. The boot manager reads a short list of start-up settings to learn where Windows is, then hands over to the Windows loader, which loads the heart of Windows, its drivers and its settings. If any runner drops the baton, starting stops there. The Boot chain view shows these runners in order, so you can see exactly where it stops."
msgstr "Starting a PC is like a relay race: each runner hands a baton to the next. First the PC's built-in program (firmware) wakes up and looks at the disk. On the disk it finds a small start-up partition with Windows Boot Manager. The boot manager reads a short list of start-up settings to learn where Windows is, then hands over to the Windows loader, which loads the heart of Windows, its drivers and its settings. If any runner drops the baton, starting stops there. The Boot chain view shows these runners in order, so you can see exactly where it stops."

msgid "What is a start-up menu entry? When a PC has more than one system, a menu appears when it starts. Each line in that menu is an entry that says where a system lives (which disk, which partition, which folder). When that system is deleted, moved, or its disk is taken out, the entry stays behind and leads nowhere. Removing such an entry deletes nothing else: it only tidies up the menu."
msgstr "What is a start-up menu entry? When a PC has more than one system, a menu appears when it starts. Each line in that menu is an entry that says where a system lives (which disk, which partition, which folder). When that system is deleted, moved, or its disk is taken out, the entry stays behind and leads nowhere. Removing such an entry deletes nothing else: it only tidies up the menu."

msgid "What is the PC's own start-up list? Newer PCs keep a short list inside the PC itself (in the firmware, not on any disk) of things they can start: Windows Boot Manager, Linux, USB sticks, the network. You see it in the boot menu that opens with a key while the PC starts (often F12, F11, F8 or Esc). When a system is removed, its line can stay in that list. Removing the line changes nothing on any disk."
msgstr "What is the PC's own start-up list? Newer PCs keep a short list inside the PC itself (in the firmware, not on any disk) of things they can start: Windows Boot Manager, Linux, USB sticks, the network. You see it in the boot menu that opens with a key while the PC starts (often F12, F11, F8 or Esc). When a system is removed, its line can stay in that list. Removing the line changes nothing on any disk."

msgid "What is a partition table? A disk is divided into parts called partitions, for example a small start-up partition and a big C: drive. A tiny table at the very start of the disk (with a spare copy at the end, on newer disks) records where each part begins and ends, like the table of contents of a book. If the table is damaged, the PC cannot find anything on the disk, even though the files are still there."
msgstr "What is a partition table? A disk is divided into parts called partitions, for example a small start-up partition and a big C: drive. A tiny table at the very start of the disk (with a spare copy at the end, on newer disks) records where each part begins and ends, like the table of contents of a book. If the table is damaged, the PC cannot find anything on the disk, even though the files are still there."

msgid "What is Secure Boot? It is a safety check built into newer PCs. When it is on, the PC only starts programs that carry a valid digital signature, like a passport check at the door. That stops harmful programs (bootkits) from hiding in the start-up process before Windows or your antivirus runs.\n\nWindows 8, 10 and 11 are signed, and so are most big Linux systems (Ubuntu, Fedora, openSUSE, Mint) through a helper called shim. Windows 7, older Windows, many smaller Linux systems and some repair tools are not signed, so they only start with Secure Boot off.\n\nKeep it on if everything you use starts with it on. Turn it off only for a system or tool that refuses to start, and back on afterwards if you can. Switching it does not change or delete anything on the disks. If the Windows drive is encrypted with BitLocker, have its recovery key ready: Windows may ask for it once after Secure Boot is switched."
msgstr "What is Secure Boot? It is a safety check built into newer PCs. When it is on, the PC only starts programs that carry a valid digital signature, like a passport check at the door. That stops harmful programs (bootkits) from hiding in the start-up process before Windows or your antivirus runs.\n\nWindows 8, 10 and 11 are signed, and so are most big Linux systems (Ubuntu, Fedora, openSUSE, Mint) through a helper called shim. Windows 7, older Windows, many smaller Linux systems and some repair tools are not signed, so they only start with Secure Boot off.\n\nKeep it on if everything you use starts with it on. Turn it off only for a system or tool that refuses to start, and back on afterwards if you can. Switching it does not change or delete anything on the disks. If the Windows drive is encrypted with BitLocker, have its recovery key ready: Windows may ask for it once after Secure Boot is switched."

msgid "This PC was started in legacy (BIOS) mode, which has no Secure Boot. Secure Boot only works in UEFI mode."
msgstr "This PC was started in legacy (BIOS) mode, which has no Secure Boot. Secure Boot only works in UEFI mode."

msgid "Whether Secure Boot is on could not be read here."
msgstr "Whether Secure Boot is on could not be read here."

msgid "Secure Boot is on."
msgstr "Secure Boot is on."

msgid "Secure Boot is off, and its keys are cleared (\"Setup mode\"). To switch it on, first restore the factory keys in the firmware settings."
msgstr "Secure Boot is off, and its keys are cleared (\"Setup mode\"). To switch it on, first restore the factory keys in the firmware settings."

msgid "Secure Boot is off."
msgstr "Secure Boot is off."

msgid "Delete or F2"
msgstr "Delete or F2"

msgid "the Boot or Security tab"
msgstr "the Boot or Security tab"

msgid "How to switch Secure Boot on:\n"
msgstr "How to switch Secure Boot on:\n"

msgid "How to switch Secure Boot off:\n"
msgstr "How to switch Secure Boot off:\n"

msgid "1. Use \"Restart into the PC's firmware settings\", or restart the PC and press "
msgstr "1. Use \"Restart into the PC's firmware settings\", or restart the PC and press "

msgid " right after switching it on.\n"
msgstr " right after switching it on.\n"

msgid "1. Restart the PC and press "
msgstr "1. Restart the PC and press "

msgid " right after switching it on, until the firmware settings appear.\n"
msgstr " right after switching it on, until the firmware settings appear.\n"

msgid "3. Set \"Secure Boot\" to Enabled. If it is greyed out: switch \"CSM\" or \"Legacy\" support off and set the OS type to \"Windows UEFI mode\".\n"
msgstr "3. Set \"Secure Boot\" to Enabled. If it is greyed out: switch \"CSM\" or \"Legacy\" support off and set the OS type to \"Windows UEFI mode\".\n"

msgid "4. If it says Setup mode or keys are missing, choose \"Restore factory keys\" (or \"Install default keys\").\n"
msgstr "4. If it says Setup mode or keys are missing, choose \"Restore factory keys\" (or \"Install default keys\").\n"

msgid "3. Set \"Secure Boot\" to Disabled. On some PCs you first set the OS type to \"Other OS\", or set a supervisor password to unlock the option.\n"
msgstr "3. Set \"Secure Boot\" to Disabled. On some PCs you first set the OS type to \"Other OS\", or set a supervisor password to unlock the option.\n"

msgid "4. Leave CSM / Legacy as it is, unless the system you want to start needs it.\n"
msgstr "4. Leave CSM / Legacy as it is, unless the system you want to start needs it.\n"

msgid "5. Save and exit (often F10). The PC restarts."
msgstr "5. Save and exit (often F10). The PC restarts."

msgid "\n\nThis PC: "
msgstr "\n\nThis PC: "

msgid "Every PC has a small built-in program on a chip on its main board, called the firmware (UEFI on newer PCs, BIOS on older ones). It is the first thing that runs when you press the power button. It checks the hardware and then looks for a disk to start from. UEFI and BIOS start Windows in different ways, so Windows must be set up for the way your PC starts."
msgstr "Every PC has a small built-in program on a chip on its main board, called the firmware (UEFI on newer PCs, BIOS on older ones). It is the first thing that runs when you press the power button. It checks the hardware and then looks for a disk to start from. UEFI and BIOS start Windows in different ways, so Windows must be set up for the way your PC starts."

msgid "The disk (hard disk or SSD) stores Windows and your files. At its very start there is a small table that says how the disk is divided into parts (partitions), like the table of contents of a book. If the disk cannot be read or that table is damaged, the PC cannot find Windows."
msgstr "The disk (hard disk or SSD) stores Windows and your files. At its very start there is a small table that says how the disk is divided into parts (partitions), like the table of contents of a book. If the disk cannot be read or that table is damaged, the PC cannot find Windows."

msgid "Besides the big partition with Windows (usually C:), the disk has a small hidden partition used only for starting: the EFI system partition on newer PCs, or the \"active\" partition on older ones. It holds the few files the PC needs to begin starting Windows. You normally never see it, and it has no drive letter."
msgstr "Besides the big partition with Windows (usually C:), the disk has a small hidden partition used only for starting: the EFI system partition on newer PCs, or the \"active\" partition on older ones. It holds the few files the PC needs to begin starting Windows. You normally never see it, and it has no drive letter."

msgid "Windows Boot Manager is a small program on the start-up partition. The firmware starts it, and it decides which Windows to start, showing a menu when there is more than one. On newer PCs the PC also keeps its own list of start-up choices, and Windows Boot Manager must be in it."
msgstr "Windows Boot Manager is a small program on the start-up partition. The firmware starts it, and it decides which Windows to start, showing a menu when there is more than one. On newer PCs the PC also keeps its own list of start-up choices, and Windows Boot Manager must be in it."

msgid "The start-up settings (called BCD) are a short list that Windows Boot Manager reads. Each line says \"this Windows is on this partition, in this folder\". If the line is missing or points to the wrong place, that Windows cannot start, even if Windows itself is perfectly fine."
msgstr "The start-up settings (called BCD) are a short list that Windows Boot Manager reads. Each line says \"this Windows is on this partition, in this folder\". If the line is missing or points to the wrong place, that Windows cannot start, even if Windows itself is perfectly fine."

msgid "This is the partition where Windows is installed, usually drive C:. To start, it must be readable: not damaged, not locked by BitLocker encryption, and with some free space left."
msgstr "This is the partition where Windows is installed, usually drive C:. To start, it must be readable: not damaged, not locked by BitLocker encryption, and with some free space left."

msgid "The Windows loader (a file called winload) is what actually starts loading Windows into the PC's memory. Windows Boot Manager hands over to it."
msgstr "The Windows loader (a file called winload) is what actually starts loading Windows into the PC's memory. Windows Boot Manager hands over to it."

msgid "These are the heart of Windows (the kernel) and the first drivers it needs, for example the ones that let Windows read its own disk. If one of them is missing or damaged, Windows stops with a blue screen while it starts."
msgstr "These are the heart of Windows (the kernel) and the first drivers it needs, for example the ones that let Windows read its own disk. If one of them is missing or damaged, Windows stops with a blue screen while it starts."

msgid "Windows keeps its settings in a few special files called the registry. Windows reads them very early, for example to know which drivers to load. A damaged registry file means Windows cannot finish starting."
msgstr "Windows keeps its settings in a few special files called the registry. Windows reads them very early, for example to know which drivers to load. A damaged registry file means Windows cannot finish starting."

msgid "The last step: Windows starts its services and shows the sign-in screen. If something goes wrong here, Windows may restart over and over (a restart loop) or keep opening Automatic Repair."
msgstr "The last step: Windows starts its services and shows the sign-in screen. If something goes wrong here, Windows may restart over and over (a restart loop) or keep opening Automatic Repair."

msgid "Newer PCs keep a short list inside the PC itself (not on any disk) of systems they can start, in order. The PC starts the first one that works. Windows and Linux each add their own line. Windows updates sometimes move Windows back to the top, so Linux then seems to have vanished; it is only lower in the list."
msgstr "Newer PCs keep a short list inside the PC itself (not on any disk) of systems they can start, in order. The PC starts the first one that works. Windows and Linux each add their own line. Windows updates sometimes move Windows back to the top, so Linux then seems to have vanished; it is only lower in the list."

msgid "Linux is started by a small program called GRUB (sometimes with a helper called shim, which makes it work with Secure Boot, or by systemd-boot). It lives in a folder on the start-up partition, next to Windows Boot Manager."
msgstr "Linux is started by a small program called GRUB (sometimes with a helper called shim, which makes it work with Secure Boot, or by systemd-boot). It lives in a folder on the start-up partition, next to Windows Boot Manager."

msgid "A tiny settings file next to GRUB says on which partition Linux lives, using that partition's id. If the partition was formatted or deleted, the id no longer matches anything and GRUB stops at a \"grub rescue>\" prompt."
msgstr "A tiny settings file next to GRUB says on which partition Linux lives, using that partition's id. If the partition was formatted or deleted, the id no longer matches anything and GRUB stops at a \"grub rescue>\" prompt."

msgid "On older PCs (legacy BIOS) the very first sector of the disk holds a little start-up program. For Linux this is GRUB. Installing or repairing Windows often replaces it with Windows' own, and then the PC no longer offers Linux."
msgstr "On older PCs (legacy BIOS) the very first sector of the disk holds a little start-up program. For Linux this is GRUB. Installing or repairing Windows often replaces it with Windows' own, and then the PC no longer offers Linux."

msgid "This is the partition where Linux is installed. Linux uses its own file systems (ext4, btrfs, XFS), which Windows cannot open. This program can look inside ext2, ext3 and ext4, read-only; it never writes to them."
msgstr "This is the partition where Linux is installed. Linux uses its own file systems (ext4, btrfs, XFS), which Windows cannot open. This program can look inside ext2, ext3 and ext4, read-only; it never writes to them."

msgid "The kernel is the heart of Linux, kept in the /boot folder as files named vmlinuz. Linux keeps one or two older kernels too, so that if an update goes wrong you can pick an older one in the GRUB menu."
msgstr "The kernel is the heart of Linux, kept in the /boot folder as files named vmlinuz. Linux keeps one or two older kernels too, so that if an update goes wrong you can pick an older one in the GRUB menu."

msgid "The GRUB menu is the list of choices GRUB shows when the PC starts (Linux, older kernels, often Windows too). It is a file called grub.cfg. Without it, GRUB only shows a bare \"grub>\" prompt."
msgstr "The GRUB menu is the list of choices GRUB shows when the PC starts (Linux, older kernels, often Windows too). It is a file called grub.cfg. Without it, GRUB only shows a bare \"grub>\" prompt."

msgid "Linux keeps a list of the partitions it uses (the file /etc/fstab): where Linux is, where /boot is, the swap space and so on. If a partition in that list is gone, Linux stops while starting and shows an \"emergency mode\" prompt."
msgstr "Linux keeps a list of the partitions it uses (the file /etc/fstab): where Linux is, where /boot is, the swap space and so on. If a partition in that list is gone, Linux stops while starting and shows an \"emergency mode\" prompt."

msgid "Windows only starts when it sits at the top of its drive (for example C:\\Windows). A Windows inside another folder is usually a copy made while backing up or moving files, or the old Windows kept after an upgrade (Windows.old)."
msgstr "Windows only starts when it sits at the top of its drive (for example C:\\Windows). A Windows inside another folder is usually a copy made while backing up or moving files, or the old Windows kept after an upgrade (Windows.old)."

