msgstr "Windows is in Safe Mode"

msgid "Safe Mode starts Windows with only the most basic drivers, to get past a driver or program that stops it from starting normally."
msgstr "Safe Mode starts Windows with only the most basic drivers, to get past a driver or program that stops it from starting normally."

msgid "Next steps: check the report below; restarting normally leaves Safe Mode."
msgstr "Next steps: check the report below; restarting normally leaves Safe Mode."

msgid "Windows' folders were found under other names"
msgstr "Windows' folders were found under other names"

msgid "On {0} there are folders that hold a Windows (its heart, its loader and its settings), but under other names: {1}. Windows only starts from folders called Windows, System32 and config, so this one cannot start as it is. They were probably renamed by hand, by a program or by a failed update."
msgstr "On {0} there are folders that hold a Windows (its heart, its loader and its settings), but under other names: {1}. Windows only starts from folders called Windows, System32 and config, so this one cannot start as it is. They were probably renamed by hand, by a program or by a failed update."

msgid "\n\nA folder called Windows already exists next to it, so it cannot simply be renamed back. Look at both folders first (for example with the command prompt) and rename one by hand."
msgstr "\n\nA folder called Windows already exists next to it, so it cannot simply be renamed back. Look at both folders first (for example with the command prompt) and rename one by hand."

msgid "\n\nAfter renaming, scan again: if the Windows folder is inside another folder, the report then offers to move it back to the top of the drive."
msgstr "\n\nAfter renaming, scan again: if the Windows folder is inside another folder, the report then offers to move it back to the top of the drive."

msgid "Windows looks for itself by fixed names: the folder Windows at the top of the drive, with System32 inside it, and config inside that for its settings. A different name is enough to stop it from starting, even though every file is still there."
msgstr "Windows looks for itself by fixed names: the folder Windows at the top of the drive, with System32 inside it, and config inside that for its settings. A different name is enough to stop it from starting, even though every file is still there."

msgid "The troubleshoot options are the blue \"Choose an option\" screen Windows opens when it cannot start: Startup Repair, System Restore, uninstalling updates, the command prompt and \"Reset this PC\". They live in a small hidden recovery partition (or inside Windows itself)."
msgstr "The troubleshoot options are the blue \"Choose an option\" screen Windows opens when it cannot start: Startup Repair, System Restore, uninstalling updates, the command prompt and \"Reset this PC\". They live in a small hidden recovery partition (or inside Windows itself)."

msgid "The troubleshoot options of {0} are switched off or have no partition of their own"
msgstr "The troubleshoot options of {0} are switched off or have no partition of their own"

msgid "Windows' repair tools are inside this Windows (in System32\\Recovery), so they can be switched on again without changing any partition. Recreating a recovery partition keeps them safe even if the Windows partition is damaged."
msgstr "Windows' repair tools are inside this Windows (in System32\\Recovery), so they can be switched on again without changing any partition. Recreating a recovery partition keeps them safe even if the Windows partition is damaged."

msgid "The troubleshoot options of {0} are missing"
msgstr "The troubleshoot options of {0} are missing"

msgid "When this Windows cannot start, it cannot show its repair tools (the blue \"Choose an option\" screen): the recovery partition is gone or empty. They can be recreated from a copy of the tools found on this PC or on a Windows install USB stick."
msgstr "When this Windows cannot start, it cannot show its repair tools (the blue \"Choose an option\" screen): the recovery partition is gone or empty. They can be recreated from a copy of the tools found on this PC or on a Windows install USB stick."

msgid " This drive uses BitLocker: have its recovery key ready, Windows may ask for it at the next start."
msgstr " This drive uses BitLocker: have its recovery key ready, Windows may ask for it at the next start."

msgid "Disk {0} has no reserved partition (MSR)"
msgstr "Disk {0} has no reserved partition (MSR)"

msgid "Windows expects a small 16 MB reserved partition (MSR) on a GPT disk, right after the start-up partition. {0} starts without it, but some updates, BitLocker and disk conversions can fail. It is usually missing when the partitions were made by hand or by a cloning tool."
msgstr "Windows expects a small 16 MB reserved partition (MSR) on a GPT disk, right after the start-up partition. {0} starts without it, but some updates, BitLocker and disk conversions can fail. It is usually missing when the partitions were made by hand or by a cloning tool."

msgid "The Microsoft reserved partition (MSR) holds nothing you can see. Windows keeps it free for its own use when it changes the disk later."
msgstr "The Microsoft reserved partition (MSR) holds nothing you can see. Windows keeps it free for its own use when it changes the disk later."

msgid "the heart of Windows (ntoskrnl.exe)"
msgstr "the heart of Windows (ntoskrnl.exe)"

msgid "the Windows loader"
msgstr "the Windows loader"

msgid "the system settings (registry SYSTEM)"
msgstr "the system settings (registry SYSTEM)"

msgid "the program settings (registry SOFTWARE)"
msgstr "the program settings (registry SOFTWARE)"

msgid "the system settings file is too small to be whole"
msgstr "the system settings file is too small to be whole"

msgid "Program Files"
msgstr "Program Files"

msgid "the user folders (Users)"
msgstr "the user folders (Users)"

msgid "The earlier Windows in {0} can be brought back"
msgstr "The earlier Windows in {0} can be brought back"

msgid "The earlier Windows in {0} cannot be brought back"
msgstr "The earlier Windows in {0} cannot be brought back"

msgid "{0} keeps the Windows that was there before an upgrade or a reset ({1})."
msgstr "{0} keeps the Windows that was there before an upgrade or a reset ({1})."

msgid "Found: {0}."
msgstr "Found: {0}."

msgid "Missing or damaged: {0}."
msgstr "Missing or damaged: {0}."

msgid "\n\nIt looks whole, so it can be put back in place of the current Windows. Programs installed and settings changed since then are lost, and personal files saved since then stay in the folder Windows.replaced."
msgstr "\n\nIt looks whole, so it can be put back in place of the current Windows. Programs installed and settings changed since then are lost, and personal files saved since then stay in the folder Windows.replaced."

msgid "\n\nToo much is missing for it to start. Copy the personal files you need out of its Users folder instead."
msgstr "\n\nToo much is missing for it to start. Copy the personal files you need out of its Users folder instead."

msgid "Windows.old is made by Windows when it is upgraded or reset. Windows deletes it by itself after about 10 days, so bringing it back only works soon after the change."
msgstr "Windows.old is made by Windows when it is upgraded or reset. Windows deletes it by itself after about 10 days, so bringing it back only works soon after the change."

msgid "The Windows drive is almost full ({0} free)"
msgstr "The Windows drive is almost full ({0} free)"

msgid "{0} has only {1} free of {2}. Windows needs free room to start, to install updates and to undo failed ones; with too little it can get stuck restarting or show errors.\n\nFirst free up space (nothing personal is removed). If that is not enough, the partition can be made bigger with unused space right after it, or with the partition after it."
msgstr "{0} has only {1} free of {2}. Windows needs free room to start, to install updates and to undo failed ones; with too little it can get stuck restarting or show errors.\n\nFirst free up space (nothing personal is removed). If that is not enough, the partition can be made bigger with unused space right after it, or with the partition after it."

msgid "A partition can only grow into space that comes right after it on the disk, never into space before it. Unused space elsewhere first has to be moved, which this program does not do."
msgstr "A partition can only grow into space that comes right after it on the disk, never into space before it. Unused space elsewhere first has to be moved, which this program does not do."

msgid "Keep the BitLocker recovery keys while Windows runs"
msgstr "Keep the BitLocker recovery keys while Windows runs"

msgid "{0} is encrypted with BitLocker. If Windows ever stops starting, the drive can only be opened with its 48-digit recovery key. Windows can show that key now; this program can save it next to itself (locked with your password), and a rescue USB stick made afterwards carries it too."
msgstr "{0} is encrypted with BitLocker. If Windows ever stops starting, the drive can only be opened with its 48-digit recovery key. Windows can show that key now; this program can save it next to itself (locked with your password), and a rescue USB stick made afterwards carries it too."

msgid "BitLocker asks for the recovery key after hardware or firmware changes, failed updates or start-up repairs. Without the key the files cannot be reached by anyone. A copy is usually also in the Microsoft account of the PC's owner (aka.ms/myrecoverykey)."
msgstr "BitLocker asks for the recovery key after hardware or firmware changes, failed updates or start-up repairs. Without the key the files cannot be reached by anyone. A copy is usually also in the Microsoft account of the PC's owner (aka.ms/myrecoverykey)."

msgid " is locked by BitLocker"
msgstr " is locked by BitLocker"

msgid "This drive is encrypted and locked, so it cannot be checked for Windows. Unlock it with its recovery key and scan again. Do this first, before any other change on this PC: start-up entries that seem to lead nowhere may belong to a Windows inside this drive."
msgstr "This drive is encrypted and locked, so it cannot be checked for Windows. Unlock it with its recovery key and scan again. Do this first, before any other change on this PC: start-up entries that seem to lead nowhere may belong to a Windows inside this drive."

msgid "BitLocker encrypts a whole drive. Without its 48-digit recovery key nothing on it can be read. The key is in the Microsoft account of the PC's owner (aka.ms/myrecoverykey), on a printout, or with the IT department."
msgstr "BitLocker encrypts a whole drive. Without its 48-digit recovery key nothing on it can be read. The key is in the Microsoft account of the PC's owner (aka.ms/myrecoverykey), on a printout, or with the IT department."

msgid "Check "
msgstr "Check "

msgid "\nIf this disk held Windows or your files, do not set it up again: a partition recovery tool (such as PATio LifeSaver) can often find the lost partitions."
msgstr "\nIf this disk held Windows or your files, do not set it up again: a partition recovery tool (such as PATio LifeSaver) can often find the lost partitions."

msgid "{0} may be failing"
msgstr "{0} may be failing"

msgid "{0} shows signs of wear"
msgstr "{0} shows signs of wear"

msgid "\n\nCopy the files you cannot lose to another disk now, before anything else. Avoid repairs that write a lot to this disk (moving or resizing partitions, disk checks, the partition order) until your files are safe, and replace the disk. Moving Windows to a new disk (Disks and partitions > Move Windows to another drive) can help."
msgstr "\n\nCopy the files you cannot lose to another disk now, before anything else. Avoid repairs that write a lot to this disk (moving or resizing partitions, disk checks, the partition order) until your files are safe, and replace the disk. Moving Windows to a new disk (Disks and partitions > Move Windows to another drive) can help."

msgid "\n\nIt still works, but keep a recent backup of your files and plan to replace it."
msgstr "\n\nIt still works, but keep a recent backup of your files and plan to replace it."

msgid "S.M.A.R.T. is the self-check built into disks and SSDs. It counts damaged and replaced sectors, read errors, wear and heat. Rising counts often come weeks before a disk stops working."
msgstr "S.M.A.R.T. is the self-check built into disks and SSDs. It counts damaged and replaced sectors, read errors, wear and heat. Rising counts often come weeks before a disk stops working."

msgid " Linux is also on this PC: afterwards Windows starts first. Linux stays installed; use \"Make Linux start first\" in its report to bring its menu back."
msgstr " Linux is also on this PC: afterwards Windows starts first. Linux stays installed; use \"Make Linux start first\" in its report to bring its menu back."

msgid " Linux is also on this PC: on this older (BIOS) PC this replaces GRUB, so Linux then needs repairing from a Linux USB stick."
msgstr " Linux is also on this PC: on this older (BIOS) PC this replaces GRUB, so Linux then needs repairing from a Linux USB stick."

msgid "Administrator rights are needed"
msgstr "Administrator rights are needed"

msgid "Windows only shows disks to programs started as administrator. Close this program, right-click it and choose \"Run as administrator\"."
msgstr "Windows only shows disks to programs started as administrator. Close this program, right-click it and choose \"Run as administrator\"."

msgid "Windows is probably inside a drive locked by BitLocker"
msgstr "Windows is probably inside a drive locked by BitLocker"

msgid "No Windows could be seen, but {0} is encrypted with BitLocker and locked. Windows is very likely inside it.\n\nUnlock it first, with its 48-digit recovery key. Until then, do not remove start-up entries, delete partitions or rebuild anything: they may all belong to the Windows inside the locked drive."
msgstr "No Windows could be seen, but {0} is encrypted with BitLocker and locked. Windows is very likely inside it.\n\nUnlock it first, with its 48-digit recovery key. Until then, do not remove start-up entries, delete partitions or rebuild anything: they may all belong to the Windows inside the locked drive."

