msgid "Create a partition"
msgstr "Create a partition"

msgid "Makes a new partition in this unused space, prepares it with a file system and can give it a drive letter."
msgstr "Makes a new partition in this unused space, prepares it with a file system and can give it a drive letter."

msgid "Unused space is part of the disk that belongs to no partition, so nothing can be stored there yet. A new partition turns it into a drive you can use."
msgstr "Unused space is part of the disk that belongs to no partition, so nothing can be stored there yet. A new partition turns it into a drive you can use."

msgid "Delete the partition"
msgstr "Delete the partition"

msgid "Removes the partition. Its space becomes unused. Undo puts the partition table back, which usually brings the partition and its files back, as long as nothing new was written there in the meantime."
msgstr "Removes the partition. Its space becomes unused. Undo puts the partition table back, which usually brings the partition and its files back, as long as nothing new was written there in the meantime."

msgid "Deleting a partition removes its entry from the disk's table of contents; the files are not wiped at once, but they can no longer be reached."
msgstr "Deleting a partition removes its entry from the disk's table of contents; the files are not wiped at once, but they can no longer be reached."

msgid "Format"
msgstr "Format"

msgid "Prepares the partition with a new, empty file system. Everything on it is erased. This cannot be undone."
msgstr "Prepares the partition with a new, empty file system. Everything on it is erased. This cannot be undone."

msgid "A file system is the way files are organised on a partition. NTFS is Windows' own; FAT32 is understood by almost everything (but holds files of at most 4 GB); exFAT suits USB sticks and memory cards."
msgstr "A file system is the way files are organised on a partition. NTFS is Windows' own; FAT32 is understood by almost everything (but holds files of at most 4 GB); exFAT suits USB sticks and memory cards."

msgid "Make it bigger"
msgstr "Make it bigger"

msgid "Grows the partition into the unused space right after it. The files on it stay. This cannot be undone (it can be made smaller again)."
msgstr "Grows the partition into the unused space right after it. The files on it stay. This cannot be undone (it can be made smaller again)."

msgid "A partition can only grow into unused space that comes directly after it on the disk."
msgstr "A partition can only grow into unused space that comes directly after it on the disk."

msgid "Make it smaller"
msgstr "Make it smaller"

msgid "Gives up space at the end of the partition, which becomes unused space for a new partition. The files on it stay. This cannot be undone (it can be made bigger again)."
msgstr "Gives up space at the end of the partition, which becomes unused space for a new partition. The files on it stay. This cannot be undone (it can be made bigger again)."

msgid "Windows can only give up free space that is at the end of the partition, so it may be able to shrink less than the free space shown."
msgstr "Windows can only give up free space that is at the end of the partition, so it may be able to shrink less than the free space shown."

msgid "Change the drive letter"
msgstr "Change the drive letter"

msgid "Gives the partition a drive letter (like E:), or a different one, so it appears in File Explorer. Nothing on it changes. Programs that remember the old letter may not find their files."
msgstr "Gives the partition a drive letter (like E:), or a different one, so it appears in File Explorer. Nothing on it changes. Programs that remember the old letter may not find their files."

msgid "A drive letter is only a name Windows uses for a partition; the partition itself does not change."
msgstr "A drive letter is only a name Windows uses for a partition; the partition itself does not change."

msgid "Remove the drive letter"
msgstr "Remove the drive letter"

msgid "Hides the partition from File Explorer. Nothing on it changes, and it can get a letter again any time."
msgstr "Hides the partition from File Explorer. Nothing on it changes, and it can get a letter again any time."

msgid "Start-up and recovery partitions normally have no letter, so they cannot be changed by accident."
msgstr "Start-up and recovery partitions normally have no letter, so they cannot be changed by accident."

msgid "Rename"
msgstr "Rename"

msgid "Changes the name shown next to the drive letter (for example \"Photos\"). Nothing on it changes."
msgstr "Changes the name shown next to the drive letter (for example \"Photos\"). Nothing on it changes."

msgid "Mark as active"
msgstr "Mark as active"

msgid "Older PCs (legacy BIOS) start from the partition marked active. Only one partition per disk can be active."
msgstr "Older PCs (legacy BIOS) start from the partition marked active. Only one partition per disk can be active."

msgid "\"Active\" only matters for disks with an MBR partition table on PCs that start in legacy (BIOS) mode."
msgstr "\"Active\" only matters for disks with an MBR partition table on PCs that start in legacy (BIOS) mode."

msgid "Mark as not active"
msgstr "Mark as not active"

msgid "Removes the active mark, so an older PC (legacy BIOS) will no longer try to start from this partition."
msgstr "Removes the active mark, so an older PC (legacy BIOS) will no longer try to start from this partition."

msgid "Make it a normal data partition"
msgstr "Make it a normal data partition"

msgid "Changes the partition's type to an ordinary data partition, so Windows shows it and lets you use it. Its files stay."
msgstr "Changes the partition's type to an ordinary data partition, so Windows shows it and lets you use it. Its files stay."

msgid "Every partition carries a type that tells systems what it is for: data, start-up (EFI), recovery and so on."
msgstr "Every partition carries a type that tells systems what it is for: data, start-up (EFI), recovery and so on."

msgid "Make it a start-up (EFI) partition"
msgstr "Make it a start-up (EFI) partition"

msgid "Changes the partition's type so the PC's firmware treats it as a start-up partition. It must use FAT32. Its files stay."
msgstr "Changes the partition's type so the PC's firmware treats it as a start-up partition. It must use FAT32. Its files stay."

msgid "The EFI system partition is the small partition a modern PC starts from; it holds the boot managers of Windows and Linux."
msgstr "The EFI system partition is the small partition a modern PC starts from; it holds the boot managers of Windows and Linux."

msgid "Make it a recovery partition"
msgstr "Make it a recovery partition"

msgid "Changes the partition's type to a hidden recovery partition, the kind that holds Windows' repair tools. Windows then hides it and protects it. Its files stay."
msgstr "Changes the partition's type to a hidden recovery partition, the kind that holds Windows' repair tools. Windows then hides it and protects it. Its files stay."

msgid "Check for errors"
msgstr "Check for errors"

msgid "Runs the Windows disk check, which repairs damaged file-system records. Files in damaged places can be lost, as with any disk check. This cannot be undone."
msgstr "Runs the Windows disk check, which repairs damaged file-system records. Files in damaged places can be lost, as with any disk check. This cannot be undone."

msgid "Set up as GPT"
msgstr "Set up as GPT"

msgid "Gives this empty disk a partition table, so partitions can be made on it. GPT is the modern kind, for UEFI PCs and disks over 2 TB."
msgstr "Gives this empty disk a partition table, so partitions can be made on it. GPT is the modern kind, for UEFI PCs and disks over 2 TB."

msgid "A new disk has no partition table yet, so Windows cannot use it until it is set up."
msgstr "A new disk has no partition table yet, so Windows cannot use it until it is set up."

msgid "Set up as MBR"
msgstr "Set up as MBR"

msgid "Gives this empty disk an older-style MBR partition table: for older PCs (legacy BIOS) and disks up to 2 TB."
msgstr "Gives this empty disk an older-style MBR partition table: for older PCs (legacy BIOS) and disks up to 2 TB."

msgid "Change to GPT"
msgstr "Change to GPT"

msgid "Changes this disk's empty partition table from MBR to GPT."
msgstr "Changes this disk's empty partition table from MBR to GPT."

msgid "MBR is the older kind of partition table (up to 2 TB, 4 main partitions); GPT is the modern kind (huge disks, 128 partitions, needed for UEFI start-up)."
msgstr "MBR is the older kind of partition table (up to 2 TB, 4 main partitions); GPT is the modern kind (huge disks, 128 partitions, needed for UEFI start-up)."

msgid "Change to MBR"
msgstr "Change to MBR"

msgid "Changes this disk's empty partition table from GPT to MBR."
msgstr "Changes this disk's empty partition table from GPT to MBR."

msgid "Change to GPT, keeping Windows and files"
msgstr "Change to GPT, keeping Windows and files"

msgid "Uses Windows' own MBR2GPT tool to change the disk to GPT without deleting anything, and makes the start-up partition a UEFI one. Afterwards the PC must be switched from legacy (BIOS/CSM) to UEFI mode in its firmware settings, or Windows will not start. This cannot be undone here."
msgstr "Uses Windows' own MBR2GPT tool to change the disk to GPT without deleting anything, and makes the start-up partition a UEFI one. Afterwards the PC must be switched from legacy (BIOS/CSM) to UEFI mode in its firmware settings, or Windows will not start. This cannot be undone here."

msgid "This is the usual step before switching on Secure Boot or upgrading to Windows 11 on an older installation."
msgstr "This is the usual step before switching on Secure Boot or upgrading to Windows 11 on an older installation."

msgid "Wipe the disk"
msgstr "Wipe the disk"

msgid "Removes the whole partition table, so the disk looks empty and new. Undo can put the table back (and with it the partitions), as long as nothing new was written to the disk in the meantime."
msgstr "Removes the whole partition table, so the disk looks empty and new. Undo can put the table back (and with it the partitions), as long as nothing new was written to the disk in the meantime."

msgid "This only erases the disk's table of contents, not every byte; to destroy the data for good a disk-erasing tool is needed."
msgstr "This only erases the disk's table of contents, not every byte; to destroy the data for good a disk-erasing tool is needed."

msgid "Bring online"
msgstr "Bring online"

msgid "Switches the disk on for Windows, so its partitions can be used. Windows leaves some disks offline, for example a second disk with a copied Windows."
msgstr "Switches the disk on for Windows, so its partitions can be used. Windows leaves some disks offline, for example a second disk with a copied Windows."

msgid "Take offline"
msgstr "Take offline"

msgid "Switches the disk off for Windows: its drives disappear until it is brought online again. Nothing on it changes."
msgstr "Switches the disk off for Windows: its drives disappear until it is brought online again. Nothing on it changes."

msgid "Unlock BitLocker"
msgstr "Unlock BitLocker"

msgid "Opens the drive with its 48-digit BitLocker recovery key, so its files can be read and checked. Nothing on it changes, and it locks again when the PC restarts."
msgstr "Opens the drive with its 48-digit BitLocker recovery key, so its files can be read and checked. Nothing on it changes, and it locks again when the PC restarts."

msgid "BitLocker encrypts a whole drive. Without the key, nothing on it can be read: not by Windows, not by this program. Unlock it before changing anything else on this disk."
msgstr "BitLocker encrypts a whole drive. Without the key, nothing on it can be read: not by Windows, not by this program. Unlock it before changing anything else on this disk."

msgid "Find lost partitions"
msgstr "Find lost partitions"

msgid "Looks through unused space for partitions that were deleted or lost (NTFS, FAT32, exFAT, Linux ext). Only reads the disk; what is found can be put back afterwards. Quick: looks where partitions normally start, takes minutes. Full: reads every sector, can take hours."
msgstr "Looks through unused space for partitions that were deleted or lost (NTFS, FAT32, exFAT, Linux ext). Only reads the disk; what is found can be put back afterwards. Quick: looks where partitions normally start, takes minutes. Full: reads every sector, can take hours."

msgid "Deleting a partition only removes its entry from the disk's table of contents. Until something new is written there, the files are still on the disk and the partition can often be put back."
msgstr "Deleting a partition only removes its entry from the disk's table of contents. Until something new is written there, the files are still on the disk and the partition can often be put back."

msgid "Recover deleted files"
msgstr "Recover deleted files"

msgid "Looks for deleted files on this drive and copies the ones you choose to another drive. Nothing is written to this drive. Quick: reads Windows' list of files (NTFS). Full: also reads the whole drive, finding files after a quick format and pictures and PDFs by their contents."
msgstr "Looks for deleted files on this drive and copies the ones you choose to another drive. Nothing is written to this drive. Quick: reads Windows' list of files (NTFS). Full: also reads the whole drive, finding files after a quick format and pictures and PDFs by their contents."

msgid "A deleted file stays on the disk until its space is used for something new. Stop using the drive as soon as you notice, and save what you recover to a different drive."
msgstr "A deleted file stays on the disk until its space is used for something new. Stop using the drive as soon as you notice, and save what you recover to a different drive."

msgid "Make it bigger using the partition after it"
msgstr "Make it bigger using the partition after it"

msgid "Deletes the partition right after this one and adds its space (and any unused space after it) to this partition. Everything on the deleted partition is lost. The files on this partition stay."
msgstr "Deletes the partition right after this one and adds its space (and any unused space after it) to this partition. Everything on the deleted partition is lost. The files on this partition stay."

msgid "A partition can only grow into space directly after it, never into space before it."
msgstr "A partition can only grow into space directly after it, never into space before it."

msgid "Turn BitLocker off"
msgstr "Turn BitLocker off"

msgid "Decrypts the whole drive, so it no longer needs a recovery key. Your files stay. Windows does it in the background and it can take hours on a big drive; the drive can be used meanwhile. Keep the PC on and plugged in until it is finished (manage-bde -status shows how far it is)."
msgstr "Decrypts the whole drive, so it no longer needs a recovery key. Your files stay. Windows does it in the background and it can take hours on a big drive; the drive can be used meanwhile. Keep the PC on and plugged in until it is finished (manage-bde -status shows how far it is)."

msgid "Without BitLocker anyone who takes out the disk can read it. Turn it off only when you need to, for example before big repairs or to move the disk to another PC, and turn it on again afterwards in Windows' settings."
msgstr "Without BitLocker anyone who takes out the disk can read it. Turn it off only when you need to, for example before big repairs or to move the disk to another PC, and turn it on again afterwards in Windows' settings."

msgid "Pause BitLocker"
msgstr "Pause BitLocker"

msgid "Keeps the drive encrypted but stops BitLocker from asking for the recovery key at start-up, until it is resumed. Useful before firmware updates, Secure Boot changes or start-up repairs. Resume it afterwards with: manage-bde -protectors -enable C: (or in Windows' BitLocker settings)."
msgstr "Keeps the drive encrypted but stops BitLocker from asking for the recovery key at start-up, until it is resumed. Useful before firmware updates, Secure Boot changes or start-up repairs. Resume it afterwards with: manage-bde -protectors -enable C: (or in Windows' BitLocker settings)."

msgid "While BitLocker is paused, the key that opens the drive is stored on the drive itself, so the drive is not protected. Resume it as soon as the work is done."
msgstr "While BitLocker is paused, the key that opens the drive is stored on the drive itself, so the drive is not protected. Resume it as soon as the work is done."

msgid "Test the surface (read every sector)"
msgstr "Test the surface (read every sector)"

msgid "Reads every sector of the disk once and counts the ones that cannot be read. Nothing is written, so it is safe on any disk, also the one Windows runs from. It takes about as long as copying the whole disk: roughly 5 to 20 minutes for every 100 GB."
msgstr "Reads every sector of the disk once and counts the ones that cannot be read. Nothing is written, so it is safe on any disk, also the one Windows runs from. It takes about as long as copying the whole disk: roughly 5 to 20 minutes for every 100 GB."

msgid "The disk's own health report (S.M.A.R.T.) misses many failing disks. Sectors that cannot be read are the surest sign that a disk is dying."
msgstr "The disk's own health report (S.M.A.R.T.) misses many failing disks. Sectors that cannot be read are the surest sign that a disk is dying."

msgid "Shows a plan to put the partitions of the Windows on this disk in the order Windows Setup makes them (UEFI: start-up, reserved, Windows, recovery; legacy BIOS: System Reserved, Windows, recovery), moving and resizing them step by step. Each step is shown and confirmed first."
msgstr "Shows a plan to put the partitions of the Windows on this disk in the order Windows Setup makes them (UEFI: start-up, reserved, Windows, recovery; legacy BIOS: System Reserved, Windows, recovery), moving and resizing them step by step. Each step is shown and confirmed first."

msgid "Windows' troubleshoot options need their recovery partition right after Windows, and updates expect the partitions in Windows' own order. Disks set up by hand or by cloning tools often differ."
msgstr "Windows' troubleshoot options need their recovery partition right after Windows, and updates expect the partitions in Windows' own order. Disks set up by hand or by cloning tools often differ."

msgid "Allow writing"
msgstr "Allow writing"

msgid "Clears the disk's read-only mark, which can stop Windows from changing anything on it. A switch on the disk or card itself is not affected."
msgstr "Clears the disk's read-only mark, which can stop Windows from changing anything on it. A switch on the disk or card itself is not affected."

