msgid "The PC does not find anything to start"
msgstr "The PC does not find anything to start"

msgid "The PC's firmware finds no start-up files: the start-up partition or its files are missing or damaged, the start-up entry points to the wrong place, the disk is not seen, or the PC is set to start from another disk.\n\n1. Look at the boot chain: it shows the first step that fails.\n2. Use the fix offered there, usually \"Rebuild the start-up files\".\n3. If no disk is listed at all, load its storage driver (Home), or check the cable and the storage mode in the firmware settings."
msgstr "The PC's firmware finds no start-up files: the start-up partition or its files are missing or damaged, the start-up entry points to the wrong place, the disk is not seen, or the PC is set to start from another disk.\n\n1. Look at the boot chain: it shows the first step that fails.\n2. Use the fix offered there, usually \"Rebuild the start-up files\".\n3. If no disk is listed at all, load its storage driver (Home), or check the cable and the storage mode in the firmware settings."

msgid "Blue screen with a stop code"
msgstr "Blue screen with a stop code"

msgid "Windows starts, then stops with a blue screen"
msgstr "Windows starts, then stops with a blue screen"

msgid "Windows itself found something it cannot go on with: a faulty or missing driver, damaged system files, a damaged registry, or failing hardware.\n\n1. Read the stop code under Windows Repair > Repair tools > Recent crashes: it says what the code means.\n2. INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE: \"Turn the disk drivers back on\", and check the storage mode (AHCI / RAID / VMD).\n3. After a new driver or update: System Restore, or remove the update.\n4. Otherwise: \"Repair Windows system files\", then \"Repair the Windows image\"."
msgstr "Windows itself found something it cannot go on with: a faulty or missing driver, damaged system files, a damaged registry, or failing hardware.\n\n1. Read the stop code under Windows Repair > Repair tools > Recent crashes: it says what the code means.\n2. INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE: \"Turn the disk drivers back on\", and check the storage mode (AHCI / RAID / VMD).\n3. After a new driver or update: System Restore, or remove the update.\n4. Otherwise: \"Repair Windows system files\", then \"Repair the Windows image\"."

msgid "Open the repair tools"
msgstr "Open the repair tools"

msgid "Repair loop (\"Preparing Automatic Repair\")"
msgstr "Repair loop (\"Preparing Automatic Repair\")"

msgid "Windows keeps trying to repair itself"
msgstr "Windows keeps trying to repair itself"

msgid "Windows failed to start twice and now starts its own repair each time, which does not succeed.\n\n1. Read Automatic Repair's own log (Windows Repair > Logs > SrtTrail.txt): its last lines name the root cause.\n2. Usual cures: \"Undo unfinished updates\", \"Put back the registry's backup copy\", \"Repair Windows system files\", \"Check the Windows drive for errors\".\n3. To see the real error instead of the loop: Start-up options > Automatic Repair > Off."
msgstr "Windows failed to start twice and now starts its own repair each time, which does not succeed.\n\n1. Read Automatic Repair's own log (Windows Repair > Logs > SrtTrail.txt): its last lines name the root cause.\n2. Usual cures: \"Undo unfinished updates\", \"Put back the registry's backup copy\", \"Repair Windows system files\", \"Check the Windows drive for errors\".\n3. To see the real error instead of the loop: Start-up options > Automatic Repair > Off."

msgid "Read the logs"
msgstr "Read the logs"

msgid "It asks for a BitLocker recovery key"
msgstr "It asks for a BitLocker recovery key"

msgid "The drive is locked by BitLocker"
msgstr "The drive is locked by BitLocker"

msgid "BitLocker locks the drive after changes to the firmware, Secure Boot, the start-up files or the hardware. The files are fine, but nothing can be read or repaired until the drive is unlocked with its 48-digit recovery key.\n\n1. Find the key: the Microsoft account of the PC's owner (aka.ms/myrecoverykey), a printout, the IT department, or the keys saved here.\n2. Unlock the drive, then scan again.\n3. Do not remove start-up entries or partitions before that."
msgstr "BitLocker locks the drive after changes to the firmware, Secure Boot, the start-up files or the hardware. The files are fine, but nothing can be read or repaired until the drive is unlocked with its 48-digit recovery key.\n\n1. Find the key: the Microsoft account of the PC's owner (aka.ms/myrecoverykey), a printout, the IT department, or the keys saved here.\n2. Unlock the drive, then scan again.\n3. Do not remove start-up entries or partitions before that."

msgid "Open BitLocker keys"
msgstr "Open BitLocker keys"

msgid "It starts, then freezes or restarts"
msgstr "It starts, then freezes or restarts"

msgid "Windows gets part of the way"
msgstr "Windows gets part of the way"

msgid "Something that loads late fails: a driver, a service or a program that starts with Windows, or overheating or failing hardware.\n\n1. Start in Safe Mode (Windows Repair > Start-up options). If Safe Mode works, a driver or a start-up program is the cause: remove what was installed last.\n2. Switch on boot logging and read ntbtlog.txt under Logs.\n3. Check the disks' health and run a surface test (Disks and data)."
msgstr "Something that loads late fails: a driver, a service or a program that starts with Windows, or overheating or failing hardware.\n\n1. Start in Safe Mode (Windows Repair > Start-up options). If Safe Mode works, a driver or a start-up program is the cause: remove what was installed last.\n2. Switch on boot logging and read ntbtlog.txt under Logs.\n3. Check the disks' health and run a surface test (Disks and data)."

