msgstr "The password is wrong, forgotten, or the account is locked"

msgid "The account is disabled or locked, or its password is not known. This only helps an account kept on this PC, not a Microsoft or work/school sign-in.\n\n1. Open Accounts (Windows Repair) and read the accounts.\n2. Locked after wrong passwords: Unlock it.\n3. No admin account to sign in to: enable the built-in Administrator.\n4. Password forgotten: clear it, then set a new one once Windows starts (this loses that account's encrypted files and saved passwords)."
msgstr "The account is disabled or locked, or its password is not known. This only helps an account kept on this PC, not a Microsoft or work/school sign-in.\n\n1. Open Accounts (Windows Repair) and read the accounts.\n2. Locked after wrong passwords: Unlock it.\n3. No admin account to sign in to: enable the built-in Administrator.\n4. Password forgotten: clear it, then set a new one once Windows starts (this loses that account's encrypted files and saved passwords)."

msgid "Open the accounts"
msgstr "Open the accounts"

msgid "\"Secure Boot Violation\" or won't start after an update"
msgstr "\"Secure Boot Violation\" or won't start after an update"

msgid "The firmware refuses the boot files"
msgstr "The firmware refuses the boot files"

msgid "Secure Boot refuses the boot manager. Often the 2011/2023 certificate change: a Windows update revoked the old certificate and the boot files or the firmware's trust list have not caught up. It can also be unsigned boot files or media.\n\n1. Open \"About Secure Boot\" to see which certificates this PC trusts and revokes, and the exact steps for it.\n2. Usually: turn Secure Boot off in the firmware to start, install every Windows update, then rebuild the start-up files and turn Secure Boot back on.\n3. Or rebuild the start-up files first (Windows Repair), so a current, signed boot manager is written."
msgstr "Secure Boot refuses the boot manager. Often the 2011/2023 certificate change: a Windows update revoked the old certificate and the boot files or the firmware's trust list have not caught up. It can also be unsigned boot files or media.\n\n1. Open \"About Secure Boot\" to see which certificates this PC trusts and revokes, and the exact steps for it.\n2. Usually: turn Secure Boot off in the firmware to start, install every Windows update, then rebuild the start-up files and turn Secure Boot back on.\n3. Or rebuild the start-up files first (Windows Repair), so a current, signed boot manager is written."

